  "content": {
    "title": "Has there ever been a tie at the Oscars? | The Independent",
    "text": "Stay up to date with notifications from The IndependentNotifications can be managed in browser preferences.Not nowYes pleaseSwipe for next articleAllNewsSportCultureLifestyleOscars shock as two films win the same award in rare tieCopy linkBookmark popoverRemoved from bookmarksClose popoverFilm Oscars (2017 Invision)The live action short film award at the Academy Awards resulted in a tie. Both The Singers and Two People Exchanging Saliva were declared winners. This marks the seventh tie in the history of the Academy Awards. The first tie occurred in 1932, with Fredric March and Wallace Beery sharing an award. Katharine Hepburn and Barbra Streisand famously tied for Best Actress in 1968. More recently, in 2012, Skyfall and Zero Dark Thirty were both recognised for sound editing. In full2026 Oscar nominees left baffled as rare tied winners announcedMore bulletinsTrump calls UK aircraft carriers ‘toys’ in latest swipe at StarmerScottish football star Graham Carey announces death of his partnerTrump will ‘go ballistic’ if King Charles’s state visit is cancelledEasyJet’s new seats will change the way you sit during flightsHow Trump reacted when Hegseth said US will continue Iran conflictAndrew accused of having ‘traumatising’ sex at Epstein’s apartmentThank you for registeringPlease refresh the page or navigate to another page on the site to be automatically logged inPlease refresh your browser to be logged in",
